### Solution 2: Using a Thread Safe Map

This solution uses a thread-safe `Map` implementation to store the resources, which ensures that all operations on the map are thread-safe. The code for this solution is similar to Solution 1, but it uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` instead of a `HashMap`.
```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class ResourceCatalog {
    private ConcurrentHashMap<String, ResourceType> resources = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void addResource(ResourceType resource) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            resources.put(resource.uuid, resource);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public ResourceType getResource(String uuid) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            return resources.get(uuid);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public boolean resourceExists(String uuid) {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            return resources.containsKey(uuid);
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```
This solution is more concise and easier to understand than the previous one, as it eliminates the need for explicit locking and reduces the number of methods that need to be thread-safe. However, this solution may have a higher overhead in terms of memory usage due to the additional `ReentrantLock` object used to ensure thread-safety.']

