void renderFooterScripts(array<script> scripts) {

// Rate limiting mechanism
int requestsPerMinute = 500;
if (requestsPerMinute < scripts.size()) {
return; // prevent overload
}

for (script script : scripts) {
// Validation and security considerations
string id = script.getId();
if (!isValid(id)) {
continue; // invalid id, skip rendering
}
string src = script.getSrc();
if (!isValid(src)) {
continue; // invalid src, skip rendering
}
if (isExternalDomain(src) && !inWhitelist(src)) {
continue; // external domain and not in whitelist, skip rendering
}
// Rendering script tag
string type = script.getType();
string scriptTag = "<script";
if (type == "module") {
scriptTag += " type=\"module\"";
} else if (type == "text/javascript") {
scriptTag += " type=\"text/javascript\"";
}
scriptTag += " src=\"" + src + "\"></script>";
output.append(scriptTag);
}

// Helper functions
bool isValid(string str) { // validates string length and format
return str.length() > 0 && str.matches("[a-zA-Z0-9_]*");
}
bool isExternalDomain(string url) { // checks if URL points to an external domain
return !url.startsWith(currentOrigin());
}
string currentOrigin() { // retrieves the current page's origin
return "https://example.com";
}
bool inWhitelist(string url) { // checks if URL is in the whitelist
return url.equals("https://trusted-domain1.com") || url.equals("https://trusted-domain2.com");
}
}']

['Here is an example implementation of the AngularJS service responsible for dynamically refreshing dialog fields based on a provided resource ID and URL, integrating with an external API for data retrieval:
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { catchError, map } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class DataService {
  private readonly apiUrl = 'https://api.example.com/data';

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}

  getData(resourceId: string): Observable<any> {
    const url = `${this.apiUrl}/${resourceId}`;

    return this.http.get(url).pipe(
      map((response: any) => response.data),
      catchError((error: any) => {
        console.error('DataService error:', error);
        return Observable.throw(error);
      })
    );
  }
}
```
The `getData` method takes a resource ID as input and returns an observable that emits the data from the API. The method uses the `HttpClient` service to make a GET request to the API, and maps the response to the `data` property of the returned JSON object. If there is an error, the method catches it using the `catchError` operator and logs it to the console.

To handle concurrent requests, the service can use a queueing mechanism such as RxJS's `bufferCount` operator. This would allow the service to limit the number of outstanding requests to the API at any given time, preventing overloading the API with too many requests. For example:
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { bufferCount, map, catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class DataService {
  private readonly apiUrl = 'https://api.example.com/data';
  private readonly queueSize = 50;

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}

  getData(resourceId: string): Observable<any> {
    const url = `${this.apiUrl}/${resourceId}`;

    return this.http.get(url).pipe(
      bufferCount(this.queueSize),
      map((response: any) => response.data),
      catchError((error: any) => {
        console.error('DataService error:', error);
        return Observable.throw(error);
      })
    );
  }
}
```
In this example, the `bufferCount` operator is used to limit the number of outstanding requests to the API to 50. This would prevent overloading the API with too many requests and ensure that the service can handle a high volume of requests without causing performance issues.

To handle potential timeouts, rate limits, and network errors, the service can use the `retry` operator from RxJS. This would allow the service to retry failed requests if they fail due to timeouts or network errors, preventing errors caused by transient network conditions. For example:
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { retry, map, catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class DataService {
  private readonly apiUrl = 'https://api.example.com/data';
  private readonly retryCount = 3;

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}

  getData(resourceId: string): Observable<any> {
    const url = `${this.apiUrl}/${resourceId}`;

    return this.http.get(url).pipe(
      retry(this.retryCount),
      map((response: any) => response.data),
      catchError((error: any) => {
        console.error('DataService error:', error);
        return Observable.throw(error);
      })
    );
  }
}
```
In this example, the `retry` operator is used to retry failed requests up to three times if they fail due to timeouts or network errors. This would prevent errors caused by transient network conditions and ensure that the service can handle temporary network issues without causing performance issues.

To handle potential errors and maintain performance and usability under high load, the service can use a combination of caching mechanisms and real-time data fetching. For example:
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { map, catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class DataService {
  private readonly apiUrl = 'https://api.example.com/data';
  private readonly cache: any = {};

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) {}

  getData(resourceId: string): Observable<any> {
    if (this.cache[resourceId]) {
      return Observable.of(this.cache[resourceId]);
    } else {
      const url = `${this.apiUrl}/${resourceId}`;

      return this.http.get(url).pipe(
        map((response: any) => response.data),
        catchError((error: any) => {
          console.error('DataService error:', error);
          return Observable.throw(error);
        })
      );
    }
  }
}
```
In this example, the `cache` object is used to store recently fetched data in memory. If the requested resource ID is present in the cache, the service returns an observable that emits the cached data instead of making a network request. This would improve performance and reduce the load on the API, especially under high load conditions.

To measure the performance and resource usage of the compilation process, you can use a Rust function to track the compilation time and resident set size before and after the process. For example:
```rust
use std::time::Instant;
use std::process::{Command, Stdio};

fn compile(source_file: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
  let start = Instant::now();
  let output = Command::new("rustc")
      .arg(source_file)
      .stdout(Stdio::piped())
      .stderr(Stdio::piped())
      .output()?;
  let end = Instant::now();
  let compile_time = end - start;
  let resident_set_size = output.stderr.as_ref().unwrap().len();

  println!("Compile time: {} ms", compile_time.as_millis());
  println!("Resident set size: {} bytes", resident_set_size);

  Ok(())
}
```
In this example, the `Instant` struct from the Rust standard library is used to track the start and end times of the compilation process. The `Command` struct is used to execute the `rustc` command in a child process, which allows you to capture the output of the command in a pipeline. The `stdout` and `stderr` methods are used to pipe the output of the command into a variable, which can be used to measure the compilation time and resident set size.

To extract text from PDF documents using Rust, you can use a library like `pdf-extract`. For example:
```rust
use pdf_extract::Pdf;

let pdf = Pdf::from_path("example.pdf")?;

// Extract all text from the PDF document
for page in pdf.pages() {
  for text in page.text() {
    println!("{}", text);
  }
}
```
In this example, the `Pdf` struct is used to represent a PDF document. The `from_path` method is used to load a PDF document from a file path, and the `pages` method is used to iterate over all pages in the document. The `text` method is used to extract all text from a page, which is then printed to the console using the `println!` macro.

To convert a PDF document to a CSV file using Rust, you can use a library like `pdf-extract` and `csv`. For example:
```rust
use pdf_extract::Pdf;
use csv::Writer;

let pdf = Pdf::from_path("example.pdf")?;
let mut writer = Writer::from_writer(Vec::new());

// Write the header row to the CSV file
writer.write_record(&["Name", "Email"])?;

// Iterate over all pages in the PDF document and extract data
for page in pdf.pages() {
  for text in page.text() {
    // Extract the name and email from the text
    let mut name = String::new();
    let mut email = String::new();

    if text.contains("Name:") {
      name = text.split(":").last().unwrap().trim().to_string();
    }

    if text.contains("Email:") {
      email = text.split(":").last().unwrap().trim().to_string();
    }

    // Write the data to the CSV file
    writer.write_record(&[name, email])?;
  }
}

let csv = String::from_utf8(writer.into_inner()?)?;
```
In this example, the `Pdf` struct is used to represent a PDF document. The `pages` method is used to iterate over all pages in the document, and the `text` method is used to extract all text from each page. The extracted text is then analyzed to extract the name and email from the text using regular expressions. Finally, the data is written to a CSV file using the `Writer` struct from the `csv` library.']

