Note that the `hot-reloading` strategy is defined in a separate file named `hot-reloading.js`. This file exports a function that takes the current module as an argument and returns a new version of the module with the same exports but with the hot-reloading behavior added. The hot-reloading behavior is implemented using the `Module` class from Node.js, which allows us to create a new version of a module and load it into the current context.

The `hot-reloading` strategy can be enabled by passing the `--enable-hot-reloading` flag when starting the server. For example:
```bash
node src/index.js --enable-hot-reloading
```
This will enable hot reloading for all modules that have the `hotReloading` option set to true in their configuration files. The `--enable-hot-reloading` flag can be passed multiple times to enable hot reloading for multiple modules.

### Automatic code splitting
By default, the server will automatically split large modules into smaller chunks based on the number of dependencies they have. This helps to avoid loading too much code at once and improves performance by reducing the number of requests that need to be made. The `automaticCodeSplitting` option can be set to false in the configuration file to disable this behavior.
```js
// src/index.js
const server = createServer({
  // ... other options ...
  automaticCodeSplitting: true,
});
```
This will enable automatic code splitting for all modules that have the `automaticCodeSplitting` option set to true in their configuration files. The `automaticCodeSplitting` option can be passed multiple times to enable it for multiple modules.

### Dynamic imports
Dynamic imports allow us to import a module only when it is needed, rather than loading it upfront. This can help reduce the number of requests that need to be made and improve performance by reducing the amount of code that needs to be loaded. The `dynamicImports` option can be set to false in the configuration file to disable this behavior.
```js
// src/index.js
const server = createServer({
  // ... other options ...
  dynamicImports: true,
});
```
This will enable dynamic imports for all modules that have the `dynamicImports` option set to true in their configuration files. The `dynamicImports` option can be passed multiple times to enable it for multiple modules.

### Bundle size limit
The `bundleSizeLimit` option specifies a maximum size of the generated bundle file. If the bundle exceeds this size, the server will automatically split it into smaller chunks based on the number of dependencies they have. This helps to avoid loading too much code at once and improves performance by reducing the number of requests that need to be made. The `bundleSizeLimit` option can be set to false in the configuration file to disable this behavior.
```js
// src/index.js
const server = createServer({
  // ... other options ...
  bundleSizeLimit: 1000,
});
```
This will enable automatic code splitting for all modules that have the `bundleSizeLimit` option set to a value greater than zero in their configuration files. The `bundleSizeLimit` option can be passed multiple times to enable it for multiple modules.

### Source map generation
The `sourceMaps` option specifies whether source maps should be generated for the code bundles. When enabled, source maps will be generated and included in the bundle, allowing developers to debug the minified code. The `sourceMaps` option can be set to false in the configuration file to disable this behavior.
```js
// src/index.js
const server = createServer({
  // ... other options ...
  sourceMaps: true,
});
```
This will enable source map generation for all modules that have the `sourceMaps` option set to true in their configuration files. The `sourceMaps` option can be passed multiple times to enable it for multiple modules."]

