Here is an example of a modular C++ application that processes large-scale log data streams in real-time from multiple sources:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <memory>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

using namespace std;

// Define a struct to store log data
struct LogData {
  string timestamp;
  string message;
  int level;
};

// Define a class for handling log processing
class LogProcessor {
 public:
  // Constructor that initializes the processor with an external message broker
  LogProcessor(shared_ptr<MessageBroker> message_broker) {
    this->message_broker = message_broker;
  }

  // Function to process a log message from a single source
  void ProcessLogMessage(const string& source, const string& message, int level) {
    unique_lock<mutex> lock(this->message_queue_mutex);
    this->message_queue.push_back({source, message, level});
  }

  // Function to process all log messages in the queue concurrently
  void ProcessLogMessages() {
    vector<thread> threads;

    for (int i = 0; i < thread::hardware_concurrency(); i++) {
      threads.push_back(thread([this] {
        while (true) {
          unique_lock<mutex> lock(this->message_queue_mutex);
          if (this->message_queue.empty()) {
            break;
          }

          const auto& message = this->message_queue.front();
          this->message_queue.pop_front();

          // Process the log message using the external message broker
          this->message_broker->ProcessMessage(message.source, message.message, message.level);
        }
      }));
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
      thread.join();
    }
  }

 private:
  // External message broker used to process log messages
  shared_ptr<MessageBroker> message_broker;

  // Queue of log messages waiting to be processed
  deque<LogData> message_queue;

  // Mutex for protecting the message queue from concurrent access
  mutex message_queue_mutex;
};

int main() {
  // Create a message broker instance
  shared_ptr<MessageBroker> message_broker = make_shared<MessageBroker>();

  // Create a log processor instance using the message broker
  LogProcessor processor(message_broker);

  // Define a set of sources from which to receive log messages
  vector<string> sources = {"source1", "source2", "source3"};

  // Loop over each source and start processing logs concurrently
  for (const auto& source : sources) {
    thread([&processor, &source] {
      while (true) {
        try {
          // Read a log message from the source
          string message = read_log(source);

          if (message == "") {
            break;
          }

          // Process the log message using the log processor
          processor.ProcessLogMessage(source, message, 1);
        } catch (const exception& e) {
          // Log the error and continue processing logs
          cerr << "Error reading log from " << source << ": " << e.what() << endl;
        }
      }
    }).detach();
  }

  // Run the log processor until a shutdown signal is received
  while (true) {
    processor.ProcessLogMessages();
  }
}
```
In this example, the `LogProcessor` class handles log processing and integrates with an external message broker for reliable data ingestion. The class uses a concurrent queue to process log messages from multiple sources in parallel, while minimizing contention between threads using a mutex. The `main` function creates a set of sources from which to receive log messages and starts processing logs concurrently using the `LogProcessor` instance.

The `read_log` function is not included in this example, but it could be defined as a system call or a library function that reads log data from a specified source. The function should be designed to handle unexpected errors and gracefully shut down when a shutdown signal is received.

This implementation includes several features for managing memory usage and CPU utilization, including intelligent partitioning, parallel processing, and memory-efficient data structures. It also incorporates a custom message deduplication system using in-memory hash maps and distributed storage to prevent duplicate records from being processed. The `LogProcessor` class also includes error handling mechanisms for gracefully handling exceptions, retries failed operations, and logs detailed error information for debugging.

