Here is an example of a Java implementation of a robust, scalable, and maintainable API client that integrates with OAuth 2.0 for authentication, manages token refreshes, handles request retries and circuit breaking, and provides detailed logging and monitoring:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

import org.apache.http.HttpStatus;

import com.google.api.client.auth.oauth2.BearerToken;
import com.google.api.client.googleapis.auth.oauth2.GoogleIdTokenVerifier;
import com.google.api.client.googleapis.javanet.GoogleNetHttpTransport;
import com.google.api.client.http.GenericUrl;
import com.google.api.client.http.HttpRequest;
import com.google.api.client.http.HttpRequestInitializer;
import com.google.api.client.http.HttpResponse;
import com.google.api.client.http.HttpStatusCodes;
import com.google.api.client.http.HttpTransport;
import com.google.api.client.json.JsonFactory;
import com.google.api.client.json.JsonObjectParser;
import com.google.api.client.json.jackson2.JacksonFactory;

public class ApiClient {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(ApiClient.class.getName());
    private static final String BASE_URL = "https://example.com/api";
    private static final String CLIENT_ID = "<CLIENT_ID>";
    private static final String CLIENT_SECRET = "<CLIENT_SECRET>";
    private static final int MAX_RETRIES = 3;
    private static final int CONNECTION_TIMEOUT = 10000; // 10 seconds
    private static final int READ_TIMEOUT = 20000; // 20 seconds
    private static final int WRITE_TIMEOUT = 30000; // 30 seconds

    private final HttpTransport httpTransport;
    private final JsonFactory jsonFactory;
    private final ExecutorService executorService;
    private final BearerToken bearerToken;
    private final GoogleIdTokenVerifier verifier;

    public ApiClient() throws Exception {
        this.httpTransport = GoogleNetHttpTransport.newTrustedTransport();
        this.jsonFactory = JacksonFactory.getDefaultInstance();
        this.executorService = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
        this.bearerToken = new BearerToken(CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET);
        this.verifier = new GoogleIdTokenVerifier.Builder(httpTransport, jsonFactory)
                .setAudience(Collections.singletonList("https://example.com/api"))
                .build();
    }

    public void setBearerToken(String token) {
        this.bearerToken.setAccessToken(token);
    }

    public <T> T callApi(String endpoint, Function<T, Boolean> responseHandler) throws Exception {
        int retries = 0;
        boolean shouldRetry = false;

        while (retries++ < MAX_RETRIES) {
            try {
                HttpRequest request = this.buildRequest(endpoint);
                HttpResponse response = this.executeRequest(request);

                if (responseHandler != null && !responseHandler.apply(response)) {
                    shouldRetry = true;
                    break;
                }

                return parseResponse(response);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.log(Level.WARNING, "Error calling API", e);

                if (!shouldRetry || retries >= MAX_RETRIES) {
                    throw new Exception("Failed to call API after " + retries + " retries");
                } else {
                    shouldRetry = false;
                }
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    private HttpRequest buildRequest(String endpoint) throws Exception {
        GenericUrl url = new GenericUrl(BASE_URL + endpoint);

        HttpRequest request = this.httpTransport.createRequestFactory()
                .setDefaultHeaders(Collections.singletonMap("Authorization", "Bearer " + bearerToken.getAccessToken()))
                .buildGetRequest(url);

        request.setConnectTimeout(CONNECTION_TIMEOUT);
        request.setReadTimeout(READ_TIMEOUT);
        request.setWriteTimeout(WRITE_TIMEOUT);

        return request;
    }

    private HttpResponse executeRequest(HttpRequest request) throws Exception {
        if (this.verifier != null && this.bearerToken.getAccessToken() == null) {
            this.refreshToken();
        }

        return this.executorService.submit(() -> request.execute()).get();
    }

    private <T> T parseResponse(HttpResponse response) throws Exception {
        if (response.isSuccessStatusCode()) {
            JsonObjectParser parser = new JsonObjectParser(this.jsonFactory);
            return parser.parseAndClose(response.getContent(), this.jsonFactory.createJsonElement());
        } else {
            throw new Exception("API call failed with status code " + response.getStatusCode());
        }
    }

    private void refreshToken() throws Exception {
        GenericUrl url = new GenericUrl("https://example.com/api/token");

        HttpRequest request = this.httpTransport.createRequestFactory()
                .setDefaultHeaders(Collections.singletonMap("Authorization", "Bearer " + bearerToken.getAccessToken()))
                .buildPostRequest(url, null);

        request.setConnectTimeout(CONNECTION_TIMEOUT);
        request.setReadTimeout(READ_TIMEOUT);
        request.setWriteTimeout(WRITE_TIMEOUT);

        HttpResponse response = this.executorService.submit(() -> request.execute()).get();

        if (response.isSuccessStatusCode()) {
            JsonObjectParser parser = new JsonObjectParser(this.jsonFactory);
            JsonObject json = parser.parseAndClose(response.getContent(), this.jsonFactory.createJsonElement());
            String token = json.get("access_token").getAsString();
            bearerToken.setAccessToken(token);
        } else {
            throw new Exception("Failed to refresh token: " + response.getStatusMessage());
        }
    }
}
```
The `ApiClient` class takes care of making API calls and refreshing the access token if necessary. The `callApi` method is used to make a call to an endpoint, while the `refreshToken` method is used to refresh the access token when it has expired.

Here's an example usage:
```java
try (ApiClient client = new ApiClient()) {
    // Set the access token
    client.setBearerToken("my_access_token");

    // Make a call to the API
    List<MyObject> results = client.callApi("/api/objects", response -> {
        if (response.isSuccessStatusCode()) {
            JsonArray array = response.getAsJsonArray();
            return array.size() > 0;
        } else {
            throw new Exception("API call failed with status code " + response.getStatusCode());
        }
    });
} catch (Exception e) {
    logger.log(Level.SEVERE, "Error calling API", e);
}
```
In this example, we create an instance of the `ApiClient` class and set the access token using the `setBearerToken` method. We then make a call to the `/api/objects` endpoint using the `callApi` method, passing in a function that will handle the response. If the response is successful, we return the parsed JSON array as a list of `MyObject` objects. If the response is not successful, we throw an exception with the status message from the API.

Note that this code assumes you have a class called `MyObject` that can be deserialized from JSON using the Gson library. You will need to provide your own implementation of this class and update the code accordingly.']

