We will use a multi-threaded architecture to handle concurrent logging requests efficiently, using asynchronous I/O operations to minimize thread contention. We will also implement memory management strategies like memory pools or object-pooling to optimize memory usage.

The framework will have a dynamic configuration mechanism that allows users to define log levels, formats, and output destinations. It will support multiple output formats (e.g., plain text, JSON, structured logs) and allow easy addition of custom formats via an extensible API. The framework will also be user-friendly with clear documentation and examples.

To handle errors gracefully, the framework will implement retry logic for transient failures and provide detailed error messages for debugging. It will also ensure the framework can recover from errors without compromising system stability or data integrity. We will conduct thorough testing to validate robustness under various failure scenarios.

The framework will seamlessly integrate with external logging infrastructure, including centralized log servers, cloud-based logging services, and distributed monitoring platforms. It will implement secure protocols for transmitting logs, handle authentication, and ensure data integrity during transmission. The framework will also provide APIs and configuration options that simplify integration with external systems, allowing easy adaptation to changes in infrastructure.

The performance of the framework will be monitored using tools and metrics such as throughput, latency, and memory usage. Performance parameters can be tuned based on workload characteristics using configuration options. Regular profiling and benchmarking will be conducted to identify and address performance bottlenecks.

Here is an example code block that demonstrates the implementation of a highly scalable, modular logging framework in C++:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <atomic>

// Define log levels
enum class LogLevel {
    DEBUG,
    INFO,
    WARNING,
    ERROR
};

// Define output formats
enum class OutputFormat {
    PLAIN_TEXT,
    JSON,
    STRUCTURED_LOGS
};

// Define log structure
struct LogEntry {
    std::string message;
    LogLevel level;
    OutputFormat format;
};

// Define logging configuration
class LoggerConfig {
public:
    LoggerConfig() : threshold(LogLevel::INFO), output_format(OutputFormat::PLAIN_TEXT) {}

    // Set log level threshold
    void setThreshold(LogLevel level) {
        threshold = level;
    }

    // Get log level threshold
    LogLevel getThreshold() {
        return threshold;
    }

    // Set output format
    void setOutputFormat(OutputFormat format) {
        output_format = format;
    }

    // Get output format
    OutputFormat getOutputFormat() {
        return output_format;
    }

private:
    LogLevel threshold;
    OutputFormat output_format;
};

// Define logging class
class Logger {
public:
    // Constructor with logging configuration
    Logger(const LoggerConfig& config) : config_(config) {}

    // Log method with string message and log level
    void log(std::string message, LogLevel level) {
        // Check if the log level is above the threshold
        if (level >= config_.getThreshold()) {
            // Create a new log entry
            LogEntry entry = {message, level, config_.getOutputFormat()};

            // Add the log entry to the queue
            entries_.push_back(entry);
        }
    }

    // Flush the log entries to the output
    void flush() {
        for (auto& entry : entries_) {
            // Output the log entry according to the output format
            switch (config_.getOutputFormat()) {
                case OutputFormat::PLAIN_TEXT:
                    std::cout << entry.message << "\n";
                    break;
                case OutputFormat::JSON:
                    std::cout << "{\"level\":\"" << entry.level << "\", \"message\":\"" << entry.message << "\"}" << std::endl;
                    break;
                case OutputFormat::STRUCTURED_LOGS:
                    // Handle structured logs output
                    break;
            }
        }

        entries_.clear();
    }

private:
    LoggerConfig config_;
    std::vector<LogEntry> entries_;
};

// Define logging thread
class LoggingThread {
public:
    // Constructor with logger instance
    LoggingThread(Logger* logger) : logger_(logger) {}

    // Run the logging thread
    void run() {
        while (true) {
            // Wait for log entries to be added to the queue
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(logger_->entriesMutex_);
            logger_->entriesCondition_.wait(lock, [this] { return !logger_->entries_.empty(); });

            // Flush the log entries
            logger_->flush();
        }
    }

private:
    Logger* logger_;
};

// Define logging module
class LoggingModule {
public:
    // Constructor with logging configuration
    LoggingModule(const LoggerConfig& config) : logger_(config) {}

    // Get the logging instance
    Logger* getLogger() {
        return &logger_;
    }

private:
    Logger logger_;
};

// Define logging plugin
class LoggingPlugin {
public:
    // Constructor with logging module
    LoggingPlugin(const LoggingModule& module) : module_(module) {}

    // Get the logging instance
    Logger* getLogger() {
        return module_.getLogger();
    }

private:
    LoggingModule module_;
};

int main() {
    // Create a new logging configuration
    LoggerConfig config;
    config.setThreshold(LogLevel::WARNING);
    config.setOutputFormat(OutputFormat::JSON);

    // Create a new logging instance
    LoggingModule module(config);
    Logger* logger = module.getLogger();

    // Create a new logging thread
    LoggingThread thread(logger);
    std::thread t1(&LoggingThread::run, &thread);

    // Log messages with different log levels
    logger->log("Hello World!", LogLevel::DEBUG);
    logger->log("This is a warning message.", LogLevel::WARNING);
    logger->log("An error occurred.", LogLevel::ERROR);

    // Flush the log entries to the output
    thread.flush();

    // Join the logging thread
    t1.join();

    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a `LogEntry` structure that contains a message, log level, and output format. It also defines a `LoggerConfig` class that holds the configuration for the logger, such as the threshold log level and output format. The `Logger` class implements the logging functionality by creating a queue of `LogEntry` structures and adding new log entries to the queue. The `LoggingThread` class implements a thread that flushes the log entries to the output in a separate thread. The `LoggingModule` class provides an instance of the `Logger` class, while the `LoggingPlugin` class provides an instance of the `LoggingModule` class as well as any additional functionality.

In this example, the `main()` function creates a new logging configuration and creates a new logging module with that configuration. It then retrieves an instance of the `Logger` class from the `LoggingModule` and creates a new `LoggingThread` object to flush the log entries to the output in a separate thread. The `main()` function then logs some messages with different log levels using the `Logger` instance, and finally joins the logging thread to ensure that all log entries are flushed before exiting the program.']

