The main loop of the code fetches new records from the database using the `getNextRecord` method of the `DatabaseClient` class. It then updates the last accessed timestamp for each record in the cache using the `updateLastAccessedTimestamp` method, and checks if a payload exists for each record using the `getPayloadFromExternalApi` method. If a payload is not found for a record, it is skipped.

Once a payload is found, it is added to the cache using the `addToCache` method of the `CacheManager` class. The cache manager is used to manage the cache and remove old records from the cache when necessary using the `removeOldRecordsFromCache` method.

The code also uses semaphores to manage concurrency, with each thread having its own semaphore that it uses to acquire permits before accessing shared resources such as the database or API. This helps to prevent race conditions and ensures that only a limited number of threads are accessing these resources at any given time.']

