This is a complex task that requires a robust and scalable solution for real-time collaborative document editing across multiple users with geographically dispersed locations. Here's an outline of the code blocks you can use to implement this feature:

1. Real-Time Communication Protocols
------------------------------

### WebSocket Implementation

To leverage WebSockets for real-time communication, you can create a WebSocket server that handles incoming connections and broadcasts updates to connected clients. Use TLS encryption to secure the communication channel and implement a fallback mechanism using long-polling for older browsers. Ensure the connection management system is robust, handling reconnections, re-authentication, and disconnections with data integrity and minimal latency during reconnection attempts.
```javascript
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });

wss.on('connection', (ws) => {
  console.log('Client connected');

  ws.on('message', (message) => {
    console.log(`Received message ${message}`);
    wss.broadcast(message);
  });

  ws.on('close', () => {
    console.log('Client disconnected');
  });
});

wss.broadcast = (data) => {
  wss.clients.forEach((client) => {
    if (client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
      client.send(data);
    }
  });
};
```
2. Optimize Data Transfer and Caching
-------------------------------

### Compression for Efficient Bandwidth Usage

Use Gzip or Brotli compression to compress data sent between clients and the server, reducing bandwidth usage. This can be implemented in the frontend by using a library like `zlib` or `brotli`.
```javascript
const zlib = require('zlib');
const brotli = require('brotli');

const dataToCompress = 'This is some sample data';
const compressedData = zlib.deflateSync(dataToCompress);
console.log(`Compressed data size: ${compressedData.length}`);
```
### Client-Side Caching with a CDN

Implement client-side caching using a Content Delivery Network (CDN) to serve static assets, such as images and fonts. This can be done by including the necessary files in your HTML file and setting cache headers in your server responses.
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Real-Time Collaborative Document Editor</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.example.com/css/style.css">
    <script src="https://cdn.example.com/js/script.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <!-- HTML content goes here -->
  </body>
</html>
```
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.static(__dirname + '/public'));
app.set('etag', false);
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.sendFile('index.html', { root: __dirname });
});
```
### Invalidate Caches in Response to Document Updates

Design a mechanism to invalidate caches in response to document updates, minimizing staleness and ensuring users receive the latest content. This can be implemented by using WebSockets to broadcast update notifications to connected clients.
```javascript
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });

wss.on('connection', (ws) => {
  console.log('Client connected');

  ws.on('message', (message) => {
    if (message === 'update') {
      wss.broadcast('updated');
    } else {
      ws.send(message);
    }
  });
});
```
3. Ensure Data Integrity and Consistency
-----------------------------

### Event Sourcing for Tracking Changes

Use event sourcing to track all document changes, enabling eventual consistency. This can be implemented by storing each change as a new event in a database, along with the user who made the change and the timestamp of the update.
```javascript
const db = require('sqlite');
db.connect({ database: 'events' });

app.post('/event', (req, res) => {
  const { type, data } = req.body;
  db.run(`INSERT INTO events (type, data, user, timestamp) VALUES (${type}, ${data}, ${user}, ${Date.now()})`, (err) => {
    if (err) {
      console.error(err);
      res.status(500).send('Error saving event');
    } else {
      res.status(201).send('Event saved');
    }
  });
});
```
### Optimistic Locking for Concurrent Edits

Implement optimistic locking to handle concurrent edits, and use a conflict resolution strategy that merges changes without data loss. This can be implemented by using version numbers in the database to track updates and resolving conflicts on the server-side.
```javascript
const db = require('sqlite');
db.connect({ database: 'events' });

app.get('/event', (req, res) => {
  const { id } = req.params;
  db.get(`SELECT * FROM events WHERE id = ${id}`, (err, row) => {
    if (err || !row) {
      res.status(404).send('Event not found');
    } else {
      const { type, data, user, version } = row;
      res.json({ type, data, user, version });
    }
  });
});
```
### Versioning for Data Consistency

Use version numbers in the database to track updates and ensure data consistency across clients. This can be implemented by using a `version` column in the database to track the current version number of each event, and updating this number on every write operation.
```javascript
const db = require('sqlite');
db.connect({ database: 'events' });

app.post('/event', (req, res) => {
  const { type, data } = req.body;
  const version = req.body.version || 0;
  const user = req.user || '';
  db.run(`INSERT INTO events (type, data, user, version) VALUES (${type}, ${data}, ${user}, ${version})`, (err) => {
    if (err) {
      console.error(err);
      res.status(500).send('Error saving event');
    } else {
      const newVersion = db.get(`SELECT version FROM events WHERE id = ${req.body.id}`, (err, row) => {
        if (err || !row) {
          res.status(404).send('Event not found');
        } else {
          res.json({ type, data, user, version: row.version });
        }
      });
    }
  });
});
```
4. Handle Conflicts and Merge Updates
---------------------------

### Merging Conflicting Updates

When two clients make conflicting updates to the same document, you need to resolve the conflict by merging their changes together in a way that results in a valid, up-to-date version of the document. This can be implemented by using a conflict resolution strategy that merges changes without data loss.
```javascript
const { type, data, user } = req.body;
const db = require('sqlite');
db.connect({ database: 'events' });

app.post('/event', (req, res) => {
  const { id } = req.params;
  db.get(`SELECT * FROM events WHERE id = ${id}`, (err, row) => {
    if (err || !row) {
      res.status(404).send('Event not found');
    } else {
      const { type: oldType, data: oldData, user: oldUser, version } = row;
      if (oldType === 'insert' && type === 'update') {
        const newData = mergeObjects(oldData, data);
        db.run(`INSERT INTO events (type, data, user, version) VALUES (${newType}, ${newData}, ${newUser}, ${version + 1})`, (err) => {
          if (err) {
            console.error(err);
            res.status(500).send('Error saving event');
          } else {
            const newVersion = db.get(`SELECT version FROM events WHERE id = ${req.body.id}`, (err, row) => {
              if (err || !row) {
                res.status(404).send('Event not found');
              } else {
                res.json({ type: newType, data: newData, user: newUser, version: row.version });
              }
            });
          }
        });
      } else {
        // TODO: Handle other types of conflicts (e.g. delete)
      }
    }
  });
});
```
