using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public static class HashUtils
{
    public static string GetMD5Hash(string input)
    {
        // Create a new instance of the MD5 hashing algorithm
        using var md5 = MD5.Create();

        // Convert the input string to a byte array and hash it
        var inputBytes = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(input);
        var hashedBytes = md5.ComputeHash(inputBytes);

        // Return the hexadecimal representation of the hashed bytes
        return BitConverter.ToString(hashedBytes).Replace("-", "");
    }
}
```

This function uses the `MD5` class from the `System.Security.Cryptography` namespace to create a new instance of the MD5 hashing algorithm and compute its hash on the input string. The resulting bytes are then converted to a hexadecimal representation using the `BitConverter.ToString()` method and returned as a string.

To use this function, simply call it with a string argument, like this:
```csharp
var input = "Hello, world!";
var hash = HashUtils.GetMD5Hash(input);
Console.WriteLine($"The MD5 hash of '{input}' is {hash}.");
```
This will compute the MD5 hash of the string `"Hello, world!"` and print its hexadecimal representation to the console.']

