To implement fine-grained concurrency control and distributed locks, you can use the following code:
```
public async Task<Website> DeployWebsiteAsync(string directoryPath)
{
    var website = await GetWebsiteFromDirectoryAsync(directoryPath);
    if (website == null)
        throw new Exception("Invalid directory path");

    using (var lockObject = new Object())
    {
        // Acquire the distributed lock
        bool acquiredLock = false;
        while (!acquiredLock)
        {
            try
            {
                acquiredLock = await GetDistributedLockAsync(website.Name);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Error acquiring distributed lock: {ex.Message}");
                return null;
            }
        }

        // Deploy the website using a background task
        var deployTask = Task.Run(async () =>
        {
            try
            {
                await DeployWebsiteAsync(website);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Error deploying website: {ex.Message}");
            }
        });

        // Release the distributed lock when the deployment is complete
        try
        {
            await deployTask;
            await ReleaseDistributedLockAsync(website.Name);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error releasing distributed lock: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }

    return website;
}
```
This code uses a `Object` instance as the distributed lock to manage concurrent deployments across multiple directories and servers. It acquires the distributed lock before deploying the website using a background task, and releases it when the deployment is complete. If an exception occurs during the deployment process, the distributed lock is released automatically.

