Here's an example of a highly available, scalable video processing microservice that integrates with a complex ecosystem of external video encoding libraries, media storage services, and third-party analytics platforms:
```scss
const express = require('express');
const axios = require('axios');
const ffmpeg = require('ffmpeg');
const redis = require('redis');
const { OAuth2Client } = require('google-auth-library');
const { JWT } = require('google-auth-library');
const { Transcoder } = require('@google-cloud/video-transcoder');
const { VideoAnnotation } = require('@google-cloud/video-annotation');
const { MediaStoreClient } = require('@google-cloud/media-store');
const { Storage } = require('@google-cloud/storage');
const { BigQuery } = require('@google-cloud/bigquery');
const { PubSub } = require('@google-cloud/pubsub');
const { Dataflow } = require('@google-cloud/dataflow');

const app = express();

// Initialize Redis client for dynamic resource allocation
const redisClient = redis.createClient({ host: 'localhost', port: 6379 });

// Initialize Video Transcoder for video processing and encoding
const transcoder = new Transcoder();

// Initialize Video Annotation for video object detection and tracking
const annotation = new VideoAnnotation();

// Initialize Media Store client for media storage and retrieval
const mediaStoreClient = new MediaStoreClient();

// Initialize Storage client for accessing Google Cloud Storage
const storage = new Storage();

// Initialize BigQuery client for data analytics
const bigquery = new BigQuery();

// Initialize PubSub client for publishing video processing events
const pubsub = new PubSub();

// Initialize Dataflow client for real-time video processing pipelines
const dataflow = new Dataflow();

// Define routes for video processing microservice
app.post('/process', async (req, res) => {
  const { url } = req.body;

  // Check if URL is valid and accessible
  try {
    await axios.head(url);
  } catch (e) {
    return res.status(400).json({ message: 'Invalid video URL' });
  }

  // Initialize video processing pipeline with transcoding and annotation
  const pipeline = new ffmpeg()
    .input(url)
    .output('pipe:1')
    .on('end', () => {
      console.log('Video transcoding complete');
      res.status(200).json({ message: 'Video processed successfully' });
    })
    .run();

  // Send video processing request to Dataflow pipeline for real-time processing
  const dataflowRequest = {
    input_url: pipeline.output,
    output_url: `gs://${storage.bucket('my-video-processing-bucket')}/output`,
    type: 'object_detection',
  };

  // Send video processing request to PubSub for real-time event handling
  const pubsubRequest = {
    topic: 'my-video-processing-topic',
    data: JSON.stringify(dataflowRequest),
  };

  // Publish video processing request to PubSub
  await pubsub.topic(pubsubRequest).publish(pubsubRequest);
});
```
This microservice uses the `express` framework for routing and handling HTTP requests, and the `ffmpeg` library for video transcoding and encoding. It also utilizes the `redis` client for dynamic resource allocation, the `transcoder` library for video processing, the `annotation` library for object detection and tracking, the `mediaStoreClient` for media storage and retrieval, the `storage` library for accessing Google Cloud Storage, the `bigquery` library for data analytics, the `pubsub` library for publishing video processing events, and the `dataflow` library for real-time video processing pipelines.

The microservice defines a single route for processing videos, which checks if the URL is valid and accessible, initializes a video processing pipeline with transcoding and annotation, sends the processed video to a Dataflow pipeline for real-time processing, and publishes a message to a PubSub topic for real-time event handling. The microservice also includes robust retry mechanisms and efficient data serialization to minimize bandwidth usage under variable network reliability conditions."]

